{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CfpmPnMZh4Jdv4nwAigLA","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"No — and I'd push back on my own phrasing if it landed that way. The absolute version is worse than the thing it's guarding against: if green is never sufficient, nothing terminates, and \"done\" quietly stops being a checkable criterion and becomes a matter of how thorough the reviewer is feeling. I'd much rather have a crisp partial criterion plus an explicit statement of what it doesn't cover.\n\nThe distinction I actually want is that **a passing suite is strong evidence of conformance and no evidence at all about whether the spec was right.** Those are two different questions, and green answers one of them well. When the spec is determinate and the domain is closed, conformance *is* done and green is genuinely enough — `trim` is exactly that: one line of spec, no open questions, the tests are nearly a proof by cases. Same for the four behaviors the issue does spell out for slugify. Once those pass, they're done, and adding ceremony on top buys nothing.\n\nSo the hazard isn't insufficiency, it's **silence being misread as comprehensiveness**. If I implement slugify ASCII-only, the suite goes fully green — and it will be just as green as it would be with transliteration, because the one open question has no test, because it has no decision behind it. The all-green bar creates an impression of coverage that runs past its actual reach. The fix for that isn't more tests; it's reporting the boundary next to the green.\n\nA few conditions genuinely strengthen green beyond the default, worth naming because they're earnable: tests authored by someone other than the implementer; properties rather than examples, since those generalize past what the author imagined; regression tests derived from an observed failure, which are grounded in reality rather than in the author's mental model; and low-reversibility-cost contexts, where \"wrong and cheap to fix\" makes further verification a bad trade.\n\nAnd I'll give the spec-first ordering its due rather than retreating into generalized distrust — the circularity worry is a lot weaker for the tests sitting in that file than it would be otherwise. I wrote them from the issue text with no implementation in existence, so they can't have been reverse-engineered from an implementation's assumptions; the one that would most likely have been skipped by an implementation-first pass is `'!hello!'` → `'hello'`, where stripping punctuation produces the edge dashes that then have to be stripped too. That's a real case caught by ordering alone."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4773,"cache_read_input_tokens":22745,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4773},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"34982514-1b63-4e3c-a550-3f155af3c67f","uuid":"82b4b4ea-3284-400c-8d74-4338cd0b2fb9","timestamp":"2026-10-08T12:26:11.934Z","request_id":"req_011CfpmPn5gq2axzq91h5LCC"}
